Implemented and verified. Report:

## What I implemented

`pig_latin.py` — a single `translate(text)` function that handles multi-word phrases (`text.split()`). Per word it applies the four rules:

- **Rule 1:** first letter is a vowel, or word starts with `"xr"`/`"yt"` → append `"ay"`.
- **Rules 2–4:** otherwise a single index scan consumes the leading consonant cluster, stopping at the first vowel, at a `"y"` (when `i > 0`, rule 4), or after consuming a `"qu"` pair (rule 3), then yields `word[i:] + word[:i] + "ay"`.

Chosen approach: single-pass index scan (vs. regex or rule-ordered if/elif) — O(n), simplest, handles all edge cases including `yellow` (y-as-consonant at pos 0), `rhythm`/`my` (y-as-vowel mid-cluster), `queen`/`square` (qu), `qat`/`xenon` (q without u, x without r).

## How I verified it

- Ran all **23 canonical Exercism cases** (every rule + the `"quick fast run"` phrase) — **23/23 PASS**; log saved at `/var/folders/.../T/opencode/pig_latin_verify.log`.
- `python3 -m py_compile` clean; fresh import + `translate('hello world')` → `ellohay orldway`.
- No test files created/modified (per task constraint); verification was inline, not via pytest.

